<?xml version="1.0"?>
<CP_Scriptfile date="Mar 27, 2023">
<CPscript title="Doyle Spirals" level="min" >
<description>The challenge: adjust 2 real parameters to convert a garden variety hex packing into a coherent Doyle spiral. Keys are set up for coarse and fine adjustments: just bring two designated circles together on the screen. On the practical side, these spirals provide us with discrete exponential functions!</description>
<text> All the packings here are associated with a small fragment of the infinite hexagonal packing. Circles with indices A and B (initially12 and 17) will be color coded for visual reference. </text>
<cmd name="*" mnemonic="*" iconname="format_increaseindent.png">act 2;Cleanse;Map -x;A:=12;B:=17;create hex 8;disp -w -c;[V]; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="apollon.png">alpha Vlist[0];color -c 5 Vlist[0];color -c 190 Vlist[1];layout;set_screen -d;norm_scale -c _A .18;copy 1;copy 0;Disp -w -c -cf Vlist;[r]; </cmd>
<Section title="The Coherence Challenge">
<text> The 'spiral' command creates a packing label for a packing with hex combinatorics based on two positive parameters a and b. (Peter Doyle first observed this nifty construction, hence the name "Doyle spirals".) Circles 12 and 19 are color coded for visual reference. The next commandI will yelp you get started. </text>
<cmd iconname="mozilla.png">act 1;copy -p2 0;spiral 3.1 1;[x]; </cmd>
<text> Your job: adjust the parameters used in the 'spiral' command to get the two colored circles to match. I've set up convenient keys to work on this. The 'a' key increases the first parameter while 'A' decreases it. Likewise, the 'h' and 'H' keys increase and decrease the second parameter. In addition, the keys near 'a' ('s', 'd', 'S', 'D') make ever finer adjustments and likewise for keys near 'h' ('j', 'k', 'J', 'K'). If you want to start over, press 'r' for reset. 

I hope there's a bit of a surprise at the end that justifies the adjective "coherent" for some spirals. (In fact, precisely this experiment led to the first coherent Doyle sprials.) 

Good luck! </text>
</Section>
<Section title="Spiral Parameters">
<text> With persistence (luck?) you may have met the challenge above and created a coherent Doyle spiral in p1. 

SPOILER ALERT: I've known the correct parameters al along, so here is a direct construction. (Press 'a' or 'h' to perturb it.) </text>
<cmd iconname="bookmark_add.png">act 1;set_screen -d;spiral 6.939 1.623;[x]; </cmd>
<text> Note the hexagonal combinatorics: every interior circle has 6 neighbors. Each circle you see is actually several coincident circles. In the next command the correponding regular hex packing (which in fact is a Doyle "spiral" with parameters a = b = 1) is laoded into p0; if you click on a circle in p1, you will see what I mean about coincident circles because several will show up in p0. </text>
<cmd iconname="list.png">act 0;spiral 1.0 1.0;scale .1;set_screen -d;Disp -w -c;Map 0 1; </cmd>
<text> Computation limits us to finite patterns, but you may treat p0 and p1 as though they are infinite. And in fact the existence of parameters 'a' and 'b' giving such infinite coherent spirals has been proven (see "Spiral Hexagonal Circle Packings" by Beardon, Dubejko, and Stephenson). There are beautiful connections to "phyllotaxis" (plant leaf and seed arrangements) and connections to Fibonacci numbers and the golden ratio. There is a huge literature on Doyle-type spirals predating circle packing.

As you manipulated the parameter in the challenge, you may have noted other coherent or near-coherent spirals. In fact there is a countably infinite number of them, and quite a bit is known about their parameter pairs a and b. For example, they are algebraic numbers. Also, a to the power p and b to the power q are equal for some integers p and q. What are p and q in the challenge problem? Hint: they can be determined from the pattern of coincident circles, see p0.

Lots to discover here! </text>
</Section>
<Section title="The Discrete Exponential">
<text> Treat the packings in p0 and p1 as portions of infinite hexagonal packings. Then the map f:p0 --> p1 is a "discrete entire function". Let's have a look at its behavior.

The next command reestablishes these two packing in case they have been changed accidentally. </text>
<cmd iconname="debugger.png">act 0;spiral 1.0 1.0;scale .1;set_screen -d;color -c 60 a;Disp -w -c;act 1;spiral  6.939 1.623;center_vert 201;set_screen -d;color -c 60 a;Disp -w -c;Map 0 1 </cmd>
<text> You should now investigate the mapping here: Zoom in and out in p1. Click some circles and look at their correspondents in p0. Take a little time before reading on. You may want to change the spiral parameters: various pairs giving coherent spirals are listed with 'spiral' in "Help".

What I hope you see is properties reminiscent of the classical (complex-valued) exponential function f: z --> exp(z). The next command provides some normalizations in p0 that may help. (These will have to be adjusted if you have changed the spiral parameters.) </text>
<cmd iconname="delete.png">act 0;norm_scale -i 46;scale 6.283185;set_screen -h 20;Disp -w -c -cfc195 1 46;act 1;norm_scale -i 1;rotate -.5;Disp -w -c -cfc195 1 46;Map 0 1 </cmd>
<text> Note that f has a "period", normalized to be 2pi*i. In function notion this means that f(z) = f(z + 2pi*i). You can test it: click any two circles in p0 that have the same vertical relationship as the two red (1 and 47) and you will see that they map to identical circles in p1. 

Period 2pi*i is a prominent feature of the classical exponential function. Packing p1 is normalized so that it spirals around the origin (roughly) and so that the alpha circle is centered at z = 1. Thus f(0) = 1. Also, the ratio of radii shows that f^#(0) is roughly 1. As with the exponential function, f never takes the value 0. Finally, if you click in p0 along a roughly horizontal line from right to left and watch the images in p1 march towards the origin. Click along any tilted line, and the image circles will spiral toward 0 or toward infintiey. This f is the discrete analogue of some scalar multiple a*exp(z) of the exponential map. 

The last two commands create, normalize, and display a similar mapping but with a more gently spiraling image. This may be more suitable for observing the exponential-like features of these maps. </text>
<cmd iconname="editdelete.png">act 0;create hex 30;set_screen -d;Disp -w -c;copy 1;act 1;spiral 2.22209 1.30493;set_screen -d;Disp -w -c;Map 0 1 </cmd>
<cmd iconname="bookmark_add.png">act 0;norm_scale -i 267;scale 6.283185;set_screen -h 20;Disp -w -c -cfc195 1 267; act 1;center_vert 2731;norm_scale -i 1;rotate -.5;Disp -w -c -cfc195 1; </cmd>
</Section>
<Section title="Tools">
<cmd inline="no" name="r" mnemonic="r" iconname="netbeans.png">spiral 1 1;norm_scale -c 12 .2;disp -wr </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="a" mnemonic="a" iconname="klines.png">spiral -f 1.05 1;[x]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="s" mnemonic="s" iconname="kghostview.png">spiral -f 1.01 1;[x]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="d" mnemonic="d" iconname="amarok.png">spiral -f 1.005 1;[x]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="h" mnemonic="h" iconname="userconfig.png">spiral -f 1 1.05;[x]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="j" mnemonic="j" iconname="metacontact_online.png">spiral -f 1 1.01;[x]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="k" mnemonic="k" iconname="icq_dnd.png">spiral -f 1 1.005;[x]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="A" mnemonic="A" iconname="editdelete.png">spiral -f .952380952 1;[x]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="S" mnemonic="S" iconname="bookmark_add.png">spiral -f .99009901 1;[x]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="D" mnemonic="D" iconname="commandIcon.png">spiral -f .995024876 1;[x]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="H" mnemonic="H" iconname="commandIcon.png">spiral -f 1 .952380952;[x]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="J" mnemonic="J" iconname="commandIcon.png">spiral -f 1 .99009901;[x]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="K" mnemonic="K" iconname="commandIcon.png">spiral -f 1 .995024876;[x]; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="x" mnemonic="x" iconname="debugger.png">layout;norm_scale -c 12 .18;set_screen -d;Disp -w -c -cf Vlist; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="V" mnemonic="V" iconname="amarok.png">set_Vlist _A _B; </cmd>
</Section>
  </CPscript>
